COMMIT 59bbad35f3378a4f0b929f3d35e0e5d156e78c60
docs(findings): F-30's permit hold is bounded, not a permanent leak

Third correction to F-30, and the one that matters most, because the severity
argument rested on it. The finding claimed that an Error out of the ingress
save leaks the admission permit permanently and that stop(Duration) can never
complete. Both are false.

Delivery.java:286 schedules the save timeout BEFORE the save is asked, and
:281 lands the flight when the answer completes. So after an Error the timer
is still pending; when it fires it calls answer.tryComplete(...), which lands
the flight and closes the permit. The real cost is one permit held for the
remainder of saveTimeout -- 5 s by default -- per Error, and a stop() that
ends at its deadline rather than at once. Bounded and self-healing, not a flow
that has silently stopped working.

What is not self-healing is the record. The answer is decided by
Ack.Nack("durable save timed out after " + saveTimeout), so the sender and the
monitoring are told a save timed out when in truth a step threw an Error that
is recorded nowhere. On a platform whose product is monitoring, that false
account is the damage, and it is why FIX-136 stays worth doing. The widened
catch decides the answer with retryable(done.cause()) at :324 instead.

Found by FIX-136's test-author while writing the red, from the code rather
than from the finding, and verified against Delivery.java:275-296 before this
correction was written. The acceptance does not change: the approved cases
already assert the stronger form -- permit back at once, no clock advance --
which fails today. Only the reason for caring changes. FIX-136's row and
packet gain one assertion that was missing: the NACK must name the Error and
must not claim a timeout.

Packets for FIX-141 (Producers.java:49) and FIX-142 (Parking.java:86 and :59,
where an Error replaces the step's failure outright rather than suppressing
it) are added here; both were measured in F-30's addendum.

